Z||000005||Zoom88||12-09-2001||02:49 PM|||| Checked all the threads I could think of, and nope I didn't find any info. I thought would help solve the problem. <BR> So how about a West Coast Tech !!! If I was anywhere close I would drop it off and let you guys tackle the headache for awhile !  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR> Thats a real problem down here where I live <BR>not many people even know what kind of car it is much less where the motor is located !!<BR> I moved here 24 years ago and may have seen a hand full of Fiero's !!||||reg||1
Z||000006||K2m4||12-09-2001||02:55 PM||||Ive read and heard the same thing about the EGR valves...i dont remember how long ago i read it but what REd says is true<p>[This message has been edited by K2m4 (edited 12-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mark Matthews||12-09-2001||03:31 PM||||Mine is doing the same thing, and I'm getting code 32 for the EGR.  I haven't done anything about it yet.  I wanna get it fixed before I go get the Echeck, just in case.<P>------------------<BR>Mark Matthews<BR>'57 Chevy BelAir<BR>'70 BMW 2002<BR>'87 GT Fastback T-top<BR>'96 4x4 Tahoe<BR>'74 Wife, not happy about cars!  ;)||||reg||1
Z||000008||West Coast Fiero||12-09-2001||04:20 PM||||Ok - the Shell Answer Man is here, for the EGR diagnostic I will send those in the thread who ned the info, the full GM diagnostic charts - hope this helps.<P> remember when replacing the IAC valve that the bore in the throttle body where the plunger of the IAC valve operates has to be near " surgicalY " clean, if not the plunger will hang up inside the bore and not function.<P> The TPS aswell needs to be set at .55mv at idle<P> NOW A BIG PROBLEM THAT I HAVE BEEN SEEING - never should the idle screw be adjusted - NEVER, did I say NEVER , I meant NEVER. if the idle screw has been adjusted to some extent it will not alow the IAC to do its job. The engine will increase in revs as the IAC will try to compensate, it is a constant competition between the two.<P> The idle screw should be set back to factory spec wich essentially is enough adjustment to where the throttle blade doesnt catch ( stick )inside the TB bore at idle.<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000009||Zoom88||12-09-2001||05:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by West Coast Fiero:<BR><B>Ok - the Shell Answer Man is here, for the EGR diagnostic I will send those in the thread who ned the info, the full GM diagnostic charts - hope this helps.<P> remember when replacing the IAC valve that the bore in the throttle body where the plunger of the IAC valve operates has to be near " surgicalY " clean, if not the plunger will hang up inside the bore and not function.<P> The TPS aswell needs to be set at .55mv at idle<P> NOW A BIG PROBLEM THAT I HAVE BEEN SEEING - never should the idle screw be adjusted - NEVER, did I say NEVER , I meant NEVER. if the idle screw has been adjusted to some extent it will not alow the IAC to do its job. The engine will increase in revs as the IAC will try to compensate, it is a constant competition between the two.<P> The idle screw should be set back to factory spec wich essentially is enough adjustment to where the throttle blade doesnt catch ( stick )inside the TB bore at idle.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> Ok, very good info. especially since I just stuck those parts on . Now the question is how do you go about setting the TPS at .55mv. Also the owner before has played with the Idle screw, how do I go about setting it back to factory  specs, and is there a particular order this should be done ? I am not familiar with alot of this stuff but I have a good mechanical back ground, with a little direction and as the song goes alittle help from my friends I am sure I can correct these items !!! Thanks for the help, it literally drives me nuts when a motor isn't running correctly !!!! <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||West Coast Fiero||12-09-2001||06:19 PM||||just back the idle adjustment screw off until the throttle blade is fully closed ( FULLY ) now, begin to tighten the screw down - at the same time, keep opening and closing the throttle to check for tolerances, you basically want the screw just backed off enough to were the throttle blade doesnt stick shut when at idle ( not on pedle at all ) you will feel a little bind if the screw is all the way open, you want to make this bind go away - for two reasons, for the IAC to function, and to prevent fracturing the throttle blade shaft.<P> Unfortunately I have only used a Scan Tool when setting the Mv on the TPS, however this can be done by using an Ohmeter tapped into the TPS signal ( curcuit 417, Dark Blue - terminal B on TPS pigtail ) from the  TPS. When you have the reading on the sensor, make the adjustment by bending the tab on the TPS lever SLIGHTLY!!!! until the desired .55mv is reached<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000011||recluse||12-09-2001||06:21 PM||||Im having trouble with my idle too.  It idles high, right now its at about 1600 RPMs.  I just had the top plenum off and i didnt see any vacuume leaks anywhere.  Before i did this the idle was around 1100.  Could it be the EGR causing this too?  I dont know what condition my EGR tube is in because of the insulation still around it.  My idle also seems to go up and down a little but only about 100 RPMs at a time.  I have played with the idle adjust screw but its backed as far as it would go (just before it starts raising the idle) where does it need to be set at?  Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000012||MrPBody||12-09-2001||06:23 PM||||Secondhand information . . .<P>At the Georgia Fiero Club meeting yesterday, a member described a similar problem with his V6 automatic.  His idle varies <I>ca.</I> 750-1300 rpm; sometimes it's stable at a normal idle.  He has observed that the IAC valve is cycling in and out, evidently causing the variation.  He has tested or substituted the ECM and most if not all sensors; his EGR is blocked-off.  He has lots of experience working on Fieros, but he hasn't figured-out the problem yet.  Still searching . . . .<P>If this doesn't directly help you, at least you know you're not alone.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Zoom88||12-09-2001||09:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by West Coast Fiero:<BR><B>just back the idle adjustment screw off until the throttle blade is fully closed ( FULLY ) now, begin to tighten the screw down - at the same time, keep opening and closing the throttle to check for tolerances, you basically want the screw just backed off enough to were the throttle blade doesnt stick shut when at idle ( not on pedle at all ) you will feel a little bind if the screw is all the way open, you want to make this bind go away - for two reasons, for the IAC to function, and to prevent fracturing the throttle blade shaft.<P> Unfortunately I have only used a Scan Tool when setting the Mv on the TPS, however this can be done by using an Ohmeter tapped into the TPS signal ( curcuit 417, Dark Blue - terminal B on TPS pigtail ) from the  TPS. When you have the reading on the sensor, make the adjustment by bending the tab on the TPS lever SLIGHTLY!!!! until the desired .55mv is reached<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P> Thanks again, it will not be until Friday before I can try the the things you have suggested. Even if it doesn't solve the idle prob. it is things I didn't do and very good future reference. Do you have any idea what type of EGR the 88 V6 Formula is suppose to have on it, maybe a part #?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||ray b||12-09-2001||11:36 PM||||pull off the wire clips on the throtle body and spray them with WD-40 on the contacks pins and holes on both sides, can't hurt, is cheap, and sometimes it does WONDERS.<BR>I FIXED a v6 that was dieing at idle that way.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000015||camon||12-10-2001||12:27 AM||||Well I'll give you another suggestion.<P>This would be the last thing on my list because how hard it is to replace but it could be causing the problem.<P>My car suffered from a hard starting problem (would start but die...unless I kept my foot on the throttle for  like 10 seconds). At first my car would hesitate when cold but after about a month it started hesitating off and on even when warm. I replaced quite afew things (plugs, wires, rotor, Distribitor cap, Cold start valve, EGR valve, coil, and fuel filter) none of this stuff fixed the problem.<BR>Well my fuel pump went south last tuesday (car completely died and wouldn't start backup). Well the fuel pump has now been replaced and I have driven my 87 GT all weekend so far and my hard stating problem is a thing of the past along with my hesitation.<P>Now you may not be experiencing all of the problems I was but it could be possible that the fuel pump is going bad and not supplying a steady fuel load (pressure).<BR>Like I said it may not be your problem but if all else fails I would look into the fuel pump as a possible culprit. If it is the pump you don't want it to die on you when you are miles from home! <P>------------------<BR>   Keith<BR>1987 Fiero GT||||reg||3
Z||000016||West Coast Fiero||12-10-2001||01:08 AM||||perhaps this will help as well, also there was a recal on the pulsator for the in-line fuel pump, let me dig that up - but in the mean time, heres this:<P>Engine May Fail to Run At Closed Throttle<P><BR>Bulletin Number:     88-6-62<BR>Reference Number:    836527<BR>Publish Date:        7/88<BR>Subject:  ENGINE MAY INTERMITTENTLY FAIL TO RUN<P>Models<P>Affected: 1985-88 FIERO WITH 2.8L (VIN CODE 9) ENGINE <BR>Some 1985-88 Fiero vehicles equipped with a 2.8L V-6 engine may fail to run after start with warmed-up engine or during deceleration, or experience an extended crank time during engine start. These conditions may be caused by residue accumulating inside the throttle body bore behind the throttle plate, and limiting the air flow past the throttle plate in the idle position. To diagnose this condition, perform the following steps:<P>1. Measure minimum idle speed at normal operating temperature, using service procedures as defined in Section 6E3 of the Service Manual.<BR> <BR>2. If the minimum idle speed (as measured in Step 1) is less than 762 RPM in drive (automatic transmission) or 862 RPM in neutral (manual transmission), then proceed to Step 3 . Otherwise, perform service procedures as defined in Section 6E3 of the Service Manual under rough, unstable or incorrect idle stalling.<BR> <BR>3. Remove the negative battery terminal.<BR> <BR>4. Remove the rear air intake duct at the throttle body to the air cleaner.<BR> <BR>5. Cover the TPS and IACV to protect from overspray.<BR> <BR>6. Clean the throttle body bore in the area behind the throttle body. Use a shop towel with GM top engine cleaner, P/N 1052626 or AC Delco carburetor tune-up conditioner, P/N X66-A or equivalent that doesn't contain methyl ethyl ketone.<BR> <BR>7. Remove overspray cover from TPS and IACV. <BR>8. Install rear air intake duct at the throttle body to the air cleaner.<BR> <BR>9. Install negative battery terminal<BR> <BR>10. Recheck minimum idle speed to specification defined in Step 2, and adjust if required. The maximum allowed idle speeds are 838 RPM in drive (automatic transmission) and 938 RPM in neutral (manual transmission).<BR> <P>Warranty Information:<P>Use applicable Labor Time and Operation Code.<P><P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000017||West Coast Fiero||12-10-2001||01:11 AM||||TPS Information and Specifications<BR>Bulletin Number: 88-6-4 <BR>Reference Number: 816502<BR> <BR>Publish Date: 10/87 <BR>Subject: TPS INFORMATION AND SPECIFICATIONS<BR> <P>Models<P>               1985-88 6000, FIREBIRD, SUNBIRD, FIERO, <BR>Affected:      AND GRAND PRIX MODELS EQUIPPED WITH <BR>               2.8L V6 CPC ENGINES (VIN CODES W, S, 9)<BR>Some 1985-87 Service Manual 6E3 Sections covering the 2.8L V6 PFI engines do not include information on the ability of the ECM to autozero throttle position sensor (TPS) reference voltage. Auto-zero can be described as the ability of the ECM to determine a closed throttle position any time TPS voltage is within a specified range (see chart below). As long as the TPS reference voltage is within that range, the system will function properly. This capability is found on all 2.8L PFI engines (VIN Codes W, S and 9).<P>When checking TPS output voltage at closed throttle, there are different specifications for the different 2.8L V6 applications. "Scan" tools having the ability to also read the throttle valve angle should read 0% when the throttle valve is closed. Do NOT adjust the TPS on engines (VIN Codes W and S) if the voltage is within the specified range and throttle valve angle reads 0%. The throttle position sensor on the 2.8L (VIN 9) engine is non-adjustable.<P>                                               ACCEPTABLE CLOSED                    ADJUSTMENT<BR>YEAR       MODEL       ENGINE/CODE           THROTTLE RANGE (VOLTS)<P>                                                                                   SPECIFICATION<P>1985      A/J          2.8L    (VIN W)        0.35-0.67 Volts<BR>                                                                                   0.55 +/- 0.1V<BR>          F            2.8L    (VIN S)        0.35-0.67 Volts<BR>                                                                                  0.55 +/- 0.1V<BR>          P            2.8L    (VIN 9)       Less than 1.25 Volts<P>                                                                                  Non-Adj.<P>1986      A/J          2.8L    (VIN W)        0.35-0.67 Volts<BR>                                                                                   0.55 +/- 0.1V<BR>          F            2.8L    (VIN S)        0.35-0.67 Volts<BR>                                                                                   0.55 +/- 0.1V<BR>          P           2.8L     (VIN 9)       Less than 1.25 Volts<P>                                                                                  Non-Adj.<P>1987      A/J          2.8L    (VIN W)        0.35-0.7 Volts<BR>                                                                                   0.55 +/- 0.1V<BR>         F            2.8L    (VIN S)        0.35-0.67 Volts<BR>                                                                                  0.55 +/- 0.1V<BR>         P           2.8L     (VIN 9)       Less than 1.25 Volts<P>                                                                                 Non-Adj.<P>1988      A/J/W        2.8L    (VIN W)        0.35-0.7 Volts<BR>                                                                                   0.55 +/- 0.1V<BR>         F           2.8L     (VIN S)        0.35-0.67 Volts<BR>                                                                                  0.55 +/- 0.1V<BR>         P           2.8L     (VIN 9)<BR>                                            Less than 1.25 Volts                 Non-Adj.<P>Copyright © 2001 ALLDATA LLC. <P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000018||stsmithgt||12-10-2001||11:54 AM||||I read about a sensor that had something to do with the fuel pump, but nobody has mentioned that the fuel pump itself can cause these types of syptoms.  It can cause an irratic idle along with it dying WHILE still being able to use full throttle to get onto the freeway.<P>My car went through this cituation.  First replaced the sensors since I haven't changed them before.  Then the tank was dropped to get to the fuel pump.  Found out it was an after market pump.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> It's GM now. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>Good luck.    2¢<P>------------------<BR>My work of art 4..s..money. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/4money.jpg"> ||||reg||4
Z||000019||btoth||12-10-2001||12:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Zoom88:<BR><B>    Does anyone know about this ? If I go to my local parts store and ask for an EGR for an 88 Fiero 2.8 will it be the correct one ?<BR>I am going to go read up on it in the service manual, Thanks <P>[This message has been edited by Zoom88 (edited 12-09-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Go to a GM dealer or a place that carries AC Delco.  The non-Delco ones that I tried where all backwards and "universal".  (Basically, they sucked.)  The AC Delco one is a perfect match.  I think it was $30ish.  Took 5 minutes max to install.||||reg||6
Z||000020||West Coast Fiero||12-10-2001||04:13 PM||||Testing and Inspection<BR>CAUTION: Do Not wash any EGR valve in solvents or degreaser, as permanent damage may result, Also, sand blasting of the valve is not recommended, since this can affect the operation of the valve. <P>Wear approved eye protection for this cleaning procedure, to avoid possible injury to the eyes.<P>Use the following cleaning instructions for all EGR valves except the digital EGR valve used on VIN C 3800 engines.<P>With a wire wheel, buff the exhaust deposits from the mounting surface and around the valve. <BR>Look for exhaust deposits in the valve outlet. Remove deposit buildup with a screwdriver. <BR>Clean mounting surfaces of intake manifold and valve assembly. <BR>Install valve assembly using new gasket.<BR>Copyright © 2001 ALLDATA LLC<P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000021||West Coast Fiero||12-10-2001||04:14 PM||||   <P>Parts . OEM Part <BR> . Price <BR> <P> EGR Valve <BR>. <BR>. E.G.R. Valve . 17110813 <BR> . $46.49 <BR> <P>Labor . Skill<BR>Level <BR> . Warr. <BR> . Std. <BR> <P> EGR Valve <BR>. <BR>. Replace . C <BR> . 0.3 <BR> . 0.5 <BR> <P><BR>Copyright © 2001 ALLDATA LLC. <P>------------------<BR>Technician, West Coast Fiero<BR>www.cwestco.com<BR>310-305-4111||||reg||1
Z||000022||Zoom88||12-10-2001||04:54 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by West Coast Fiero:<BR><B>   <P>Parts . OEM Part <BR> . Price <BR> <P> EGR Valve <BR>. <BR>. E.G.R. Valve . 17110813 <BR> . $46.49 <BR> <P>Labor . Skill<BR>Level <BR> . Warr. <BR> . Std. <BR> <P> EGR Valve <BR>. <BR>. Replace . C <BR> . 0.3 <BR> . 0.5 <BR> <P><BR>Copyright © 2001 ALLDATA LLC. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>  Once again thanks for the info. Everyone has been a great help.....now I just need time to implement these ideas and info. Have the day off Fri. hope the weather holds up !!<BR> To much work and to little time for the Fiero  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  I still need desparately to replace the inner tie-rod and bushing I got from Rodney, and replace the ignition lock and wire the trunk realease, replace the the lower door hinges.........better quit thinking about it I'm already exhausted !!<P>   Thanks again everyone !!<P><BR>||||reg||1
